  "details": "Currently, it is recommended to do OcclusionCulling when creating a world.\nCertainly, OcclusionCulling has reduced the drawing load.\nHowever, hiding undrawn objects can make FPS even better.\nThis will be difficult to experience in a narrow world.\nI am creating a wide open world called Pandora.\nTerrain is optimized for Unity2018.\nHowever, with VRC, it seems that the DrawInstanced function that relies on the graphic board for Terrain drawing is not being used 100%.\nDespite the fact that the trees planted in large quantities in front are being OcclusionCulling by the mountains,\nFPS can be improved by hiding the front terrain by interacting triggers.\nIn other words, doesn't it mean that even if an object is OcclusionCulling, it only exists there and internal processing is occurring?\nOcclusionCulling objects do not need this internal processing.\nAnd when the DrawInstanced feature of Unity 2018's Terrain comes into full effect, VRChat re-opens the door to a fantastic world of imagination.\nPlease check it.\nIf you want to test this out, visit the open-beta world I'm working on, Pandora ep.4-Twilight.\nA debug switch that hides each area is placed near the spawn point.\nThis world is still under development. Not published. If you want a World ID, email us.\nIf you need a better understanding of the world and how it works, we will send you further information via email.\nPlease consider it.",
